  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nPleasant conditions will prevail across the Northland for the \nrest of today, though elevated wildfire smoke drifting overhead \nwill maintain hazy skies. Weather conditions remain mostly quiet\nthrough the evening, but atmospheric moisture and light winds \nwill allow patchy fog to develop after midnight. Fog will be \nconcentrated mainly across portions of the Minnesota Arrowhead \nand northwest Wisconsin, where some patches of dense fog with \nvisibilities under one mile are possible during the early \nmorning hours.\n\nEarly morning fog will slowly dissipate on Wednesday, giving way to\npartly sunny skies. Guidance shows potential for isolated\nthunderstorm development, particularly along Highway 2 and\nsouthward. Low level lapse rates will be high, but this layer \nis also shallow overall. This will lead to little instability \noverall for the storms to tap into. Lingering dry air will also \nprevent some precipitation from reaching the ground. Finally, high-\nresolution forecast models vary on convective coverage, but of \nstorms that do materialize, a few could become strong to \nsevere. The main threats with the strongest storms will be \nlocalized damaging wind gusts up to 60 mph and hail up to one \ninch in diameter.\n\nAny lingering convection will taper off early Thursday morning,\ngiving way to high pressure building across the Upper Midwest. \nThis surface ridge will deliver quiet weather once again along \nwith drier air, resulting in mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies\nacross the region. These conditions will linger into Friday \ndaytime under partly sunny skies.\n\nWeather patterns will transition Friday night as an upper-level trough\nmoves over the region, tapping into moisture drawn from the \nsouth. Cloud cover will steadily increase, setting the stage for\nwidespread precipitation across the Northland. Showers and \nembedded thunderstorms will spread into the area late Friday \nnight and continue through Saturday as dynamic low pressure \nmoves directly across the region. Storms are not expected to be\nsevere at this time.\n\nPrecipitation will taper off from west to east Saturday night as the\nstorm system departs to the east. High pressure will build back into\nthe Upper Midwest on Sunday, leading to decreasing cloud cover and a\nreturn to partly sunny skies. Dry and comfortable weather will\npersist across Northeast Minnesota and Northwest Wisconsin for\nthe new work week.",
